        "title": "The other population crisis",
        "text": "A  For decades, international thinking has been dominated by a principle that nobody questions: we live in an overcrowded world, full of billions of people who are destined to choke our cities and squeeze our planet dry of its precious resources. Our species is steadily wrecking the Earth: flooding valleys, cutting down forests and destroying the habitats of animals and plants faster than scientists can classify them. Our future, it seems, is destined to be nasty, brutish and cramped.\n\nB  Or is it? Now population analysts have suddenly started to question the 'self-evident' truth that we are destined, in the end, to drown under our own weight. While they accept that populations will continue to rise, they point out that this rise will not be nearly as steep or as long-lasting as was once feared. They even claim they can imagine the day when world population numbers will peak and begin to fall.\n\nC  As evidence, statisticians point to a simple, stark fact: people are having fewer and fewer children. In the 1970s, global fertility rates stood at about six children per woman. Today the average is 2.9 and falling. Such a rate will still see the world's population increase to nine billion by 2050, a rise of fifty per cent on today's figure. That is not good news for the planet, but it is far less alarming than the forecasts of fifteen billion that were once being made. More importantly, statisticians predict that after 2050 the number of humans will go down. These trends raise two key questions. Why has the rise in world populations started to die out so dramatically? And what will be the consequences of this decline?\n\nD  The answers to the first question depend largely on where you look. In Europe, for example, couples will have only one or two children when they might have had three or four in the past. There are various reasons for this. Women now have their own career options, and are no longer seen as failures if they do not marry and have children in their twenties or thirties. This has taken a large number of women out of the pool of potential mothers. In addition, parents have ambitions for their children — choices that were not available to earlier generations but which cost money, such as higher education and travel. These and other pressures have reduced the average birth rate in European countries to 1.4 per couple. Since a country needs a birth rate of 2.1 to keep its numbers steady, it is clear that in the long term there will be fewer Europeans.\n\nE  In other countries, the causes of falling numbers are more varied and more alarming. Russia's population is dropping by almost 750,000 people a year. The causes are alcoholism, the breakdown of the public health service, and industrial pollution that has had a disastrous effect on men's fertility. In China, the state enforces limits on the number of children a family may have, and its population is expected to peak at 1.5 billion by 2019 and then fall steeply. Some analysts suggest the country could lose twenty to thirty per cent of its population every generation. There is also the movement away from the countryside, a journey that is happening across the globe. Soon half the world's population will live in towns and cities. But in cities, children become a cost rather than a help in working the land, and again there is growing pressure on people to have smaller families.\n\nF  The effects of all this are harder to judge. In Europe, demographers forecast a large drop in the number of people who will work and earn money, while the number of older people, who need support and help, will soar. So a British politician's call that it is the patriotic duty of women to have children makes sense: there will be no workforce if people do not have children. At present the median age is twenty-six; within a hundred years, if current trends continue, it will have doubled. More and more old people will have to be supported by fewer and fewer young people. In China, the problem is worse. Most young Chinese adults have no brothers or sisters and face the prospect of having to care for two parents and four grandparents on their own. Pensions and incomes simply cannot rise fast enough to deal with the crisis.\n\nG  Some people hold on to the hope that it is possible to have a lively economy without a growing population, but mainstream economists are pessimistic. On the other hand, it is clear that fewer people can only be good for the planet in the long term. Until we stop the spread of our own species, the destruction of the last great wildernesses, such as the Amazon, will continue. Just after the last Ice Age, there were only a few hundred thousand humans on Earth. Since then the population has grown ten-thousand-fold. Such a growth rate, and our imperfect attempts to control it, are bound to lead us into an uncertain future."
